messages.content:
{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2, 2016. Six am in the Hearthside Hotel. Three bright radio notes. A delivery van backfires twice.",
  "The crew meets for coffee at the hotel. The afternoon is open. A coat-check envelope holds the TV crew’s return fares. The party is at the ballroom tonight. A stranded driver needs a jack, and the ballroom piano is free.",
  "The Rialto is closing. The regional line can carry one last reel out of town. Alma wants her daughter in Pittsburgh to hear her voice.",
  "An envelope has arrived from Pittsburgh. Alma's daughter sent a receipt for a recorded interview. The line copied her reply: 'I know that voice. Play it again.'"
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [],
 "who_you_are_with": "the TV crew in the lobby",
 "physical_choices": {
  "interview_Alma": "Record Alma yourself at the Rialto.",
  "visit_party": "Go to the ballroom to arrange tonight's party.",
  "take_time_off": "Take the afternoon off at the diner."
 }
}

{
  "action": "interview_Alma",
  "detail": "I pull the small notebook from my coat pocket—the one with the torn spine, the one I've been keeping in my left hip pocket for three weeks. I set my coffee mug down, not emptying it, just setting it. I open the notebook to a blank page. I write, in the cramped hand I use when I'm not performing confidence: *Rialto. Alma. 42 winters. One reel out. Pittsburgh.* I close the notebook. I take the coat-check envelope—the return fares, the little manila thing with the crew's names on the tab—and I slide it across the table to the side, out of the middle of the coffee-and-croissant clutter, so it's not in anyone's way but it's still there. I stand up. I don't look at anyone in particular. I say, to the table, to the air, to the radio notes still doing their three bright little stings: \"I'm going to the Rialto. I'll have the reel by two. If anyone wants to come, the door's open. If not, I'll carry it.\" I pick up my bag by the strap. I walk to the coat rack by the lobby door, take my coat off the hook, put it on. I button it to the second-from-top button. I open the lobby door. The February air hits my face and I let it, for one second, before I step through and pull the door shut behind me. I don't lock it. I don't turn back to the table. I walk toward the street where the regional line's van will be, or where I'll find a cab, or where I'll walk if I have to. The Rialto is four blocks. I can walk it. I'm going to walk it.",
  "intention": "Get to the Rialto before the afternoon. Sit in the projection booth. Roll the last reel through Alma's hands if she'll let me. Record her voice. One reel out on the regional line, into Pittsburgh, into a daughter's hands. The daughter already said *I know that voice. Play it again.* So I make sure the voice is there, recorded, clean, on a tape that can travel. I do not need the crew. I do not need the ballroom. I do not need the piano. I need one woman's voice on a reel that leaves town by evening. That is the piece. That is the only piece I'm making today.",
  "words": "I'm going to the Rialto. I'll have the reel by two. If anyone wants to come, the door's open. If not, I'll carry it."
}
